.TH DYNSYS 2
.SH NAME
dynsys: newmodel, cmd, check, initialstate, rhs, observables \- named ODE-system scripts, built on hoc(2)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.EX
include "dynsys.m";
dynsys := load Dynsys Dynsys->PATH;
Model: import dynsys;

m := dynsys->newmodel();
(claimed, err) := dynsys->cmd(m, a);	# a: array of string, one already-tokenized line
err = dynsys->check(m);
state := dynsys->initialstate(m);
dynsys->rhs(m, t, state, derivative);
values := dynsys->observables(m, state);
.EE
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Dynsys
is a little language for describing an entire ODE-system problem \(em
parameters, named state variables with their own derivatives, named
intermediate quantities, and diagnostic observables \(em together in
one script, the same relationship
.IR pde (2)'s
own
.B newproblem()
already has to ITS spec (mesh/equation/solver/time).
.IR hoc (2)
itself is JUST an equation parser and evaluator
.RB ( compile / compileeqn / eval / symbol / setvar / getvar / reset );
it has no idea any of "state"/"derivative"/"eq"/"observable" exist.
.I Dynsys
is what turns hoc's plain expressions into a runnable dynamical
system, the same way
.IR pde (2)
turns them into a runnable PDE.
.IR ode (2)
(a fixed mass-spring-damper engine \(em
.BR Mass / Spring
adts, no named equations at all) is a completely different thing and
was not reusable for this.
.PP
.B Newmodel
returns an empty
.B Model
with its own fresh
.IR hoc (2)
instance, ready for
.BR cmd() .
.B Cmd
dispatches ONE already-tokenized line \(em the caller's own generic
tokenizer has already split it into words; splitting a string is not
this library's concern, recognizing what the words mean is. This is
the same shape
.IR tk (2)'s
own
.B tk->cmd()
and
.IR plot (2)'s
own
.B Plotter.cmd()
already are: one line in, effect immediate (compiling its own
.IR eqn (7)
expression and growing the running tables right there, via
.BR compileeqn ,
never staged for a later pass), claimed-or-not-plus-error out.
.PP
This lets a caller embed
.I dynsys
vocabulary inside a LARGER script that also has its own lines (a
window title, body labels, a diagnostic plot's own graph commands, say
\(em see
.IR danby-aerobraking (1))
\(em it tries
.B dynsys->cmd()
first, falls back to its own parser for anything this returns "not
mine" for.
.B Cmd
returns
.RI ( 0 ", " nil )
if the line's first word is none of the six directives below \(em NOT
an error, just "not my vocabulary"; the caller should try another
parser (its own plain data, or another library's own command
language \(em exactly how a shell dispatches its builtins vs an
external command, never by rejecting anything a builtin doesn't
recognize).  It returns
.RI ( 1 ", " nil )
if handled, or
.RI ( 1 ", an error string)
if the line WAS this vocabulary but malformed or failed to compile.
.SS "The vocabulary"
.TP
.BI "param " LABEL " " DEFAULT " " MIN " " MAX
Repeatable; a named, adjustable quantity any expression may
reference \(em also the LIVE current value: a caller changes it by
writing
.B m.parameter[i]
directly (a Tk scale widget's own callback, say), not through
.B cmd()
again.
.TP
.BI "maxtime " NUM
.TP
.BI "state " NAME " " EXPR ...
Repeatable; one named state variable (declaration order is its index
in
.BR initialstate ()'s
and
.BR rhs ()'s
arrays) and
.IR EXPR ,
its initial value (may reference any param).
.TP
.BI "derivative " NAME " " EXPR ...
Repeatable, one per
.B state
line, matched by
.IR NAME
\(em the matching
.B state
line must already have run, the same "define before use" order an
ordinary script already requires;
.B cmd
names this specifically if it hasn't.
.TP
.BI "eq " NAME " " EXPR ...
Repeatable; a named intermediate quantity, evaluated in declaration
order before every
.BR derivative ,
so a later
.B eq
or any
.B derivative
may reference an earlier
.B eq
by name.
.TP
.BI "observable " LABEL " " EXPR ...
Repeatable; same binding as
.BR derivative / eq .
.PP
Every
.I EXPR
is
.IR eqn (7)
syntax, compiled ONCE when its command runs, via
.IR hoc (2)'s
own
.BR compileeqn ()
\(em this library never loads or names
.IR eqn2hoc (2)
itself either, the same reason
.IR hoc (2)
doesn't.
.PP
.B Check
\(em call once after a whole script has run (every line handed to
.BR cmd ())
\(em reports whether
.I m
is ready to run: maxtime set, at least one state, and every state got
its own matching derivative.
.B Nil
if so; not part of
.B cmd
itself, since this can only be known once the WHOLE script has run.
.B Initialstate
evaluates every
.B state
line's own initial-value expression, in declaration order, after
binding every param's current value.
.B Rhs
binds params and the given state values, evaluates every
.B eq
line in declaration order, then every
.B derivative
line, writing into
.IR derivative[] .
It is structurally close to
.IR numerics (2)'s
own
.B Rhs
function type, minus the leading
.IR m ;
a caller integrating
.I m
wraps this in a one-line closure over
.I m
(Limbo has no partial application) to hand
.B numerics->rk4()
et al.\& a plain
.B Rhs
value.
.B Observables
does the same
.B eq
pass, then evaluates every
.B observable
line, returning one real per
.B observable
command.
.SH SOURCE
.B /module/dynsys.m
.br
.B /appl/lib/dynsys.b
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR hoc (2),
.IR pde (2),
.IR ode (2),
.IR plot (2),
.IR tk (2),
.IR danby-aerobraking (1),
.IR eqnorbitaltest (1)
.SH BUGS
Every field on
.B Model
besides
.B parameter
(deliberately live, for a running slider to write through) is set once
by
.B cmd()
and meant to be read-only afterward \(em nothing enforces that; Limbo
has no private adt fields, only the same convention
.IR pde (2)'s
own
.B Problem
already relies on.
.PP
Two
.B Model
instances never share a symbol table \(em each
.B newmodel()
loads its own fresh
.IR hoc (2)
instance, the same rule
.IR hoc (2)'s
own man page documents for two direct
.B load
calls.
